===================================================
Command line tool for plotting DRT data

Benjamin Helfrecht
benjamin.helfrecht@pnnl.gov
===================================================

File contents
-------------
- pyDRTtools
  Directory containing the Python modules required
  to run the DRT analyses

Usage
-----
In order to run the script, you will need to install Python
along several external packages. 
You can install a minimally functional version of
Python on Windows through the Microsoft Store; you'll likely
want Python 3.10, the most recent release.
Once Python has been installed on your system,
the additional packages can be installed through pip.
Open the Windows command prompt and type:

    pip install numpy scipy matplotlib pandas pyqt5 cvxpy

to install the required external packages.

For the moment, the script is purely a command line tool.
To run the script, use the command prompt to navigate
to the folder containing the script
using the `cd` command, by typing "cd" followed by the
path to the directory, e.g.,

    cd C:\this\is\the\path\to\the\folder

I think you can also just type "cd" followed by a space and then
drag the relevant folder into the command prompt window to change directory
to that folder as well. Once you have changed to the folder containing the Python
script from the command prompt, you can 
call the script from the command line as follows:

    python headless_pyDRTtools.py input_dir [options]

where "input_dir" is the filepath of the directory containing the input data
for the DRT analysis. When executed, the script will search the "input_dir"
for all of the files with a .csv extension. These CSV files must contain
three columns of data: frequency, real Z, and imaginary Z, in that order.
The "parse_EIS.py" script generates such CSV files when constructing the Nyquist plots.
The DRT script also takes several options:

--plot_types
    Types of plots that will be generated and saved as PNG files.
    Multiple plot types may be specified, and the options are
    "eis", "magnitude", "phase", "re", "im", "re_residual", "im_residual", "drt", and "score"
    ("score" is only for DRT runs with the BHT method).
    When multiple plot types are specified, a plot is generated for each specified type.

--run_types
    Types of DRT analysis to perform. Multiple types may be specified.
    Possible options are "simple", "bayesian", and "bht".

The remaining options are consistent with that you find in the GUI for the DRT program:

--export_types
    Data to export as CSV. Options are to export the DRT and/or EIS data
    with the options "drt" and "eis", respectively. More than one option may be provided.
    These exports are in addition to those generated alongside the plots.

--rbf_type
    Kernel type used in the analysis. Options are "gaussian", "c0", "c2", "c4", "c6", "inverse_quadratic",
    "inverse_quadric", and "cauchy". (This is the same as the "Method of Discretization" setting in the GUI).

--data
    Type of data to use for the analysis. Options are "re", "im", and "combined".
    (This is the same as the "Data Used" setting in the GUI).

--derivative
    Order of the regularization derivative to use in the analysis. Options are "1" or "2"
    for first- and second-order derivatives. (This is the same as the "Regularization Derivative"
    setting in the GUI).

--inductance
    How to treat the inductance during the fit. Options are "with", "without", and "discard"
    to fit with the inductance, fit without the inductance, or discard the inductance data.
    (This is the same as the "Inductance Included" setting in the GUI).

--regularization
    Regularization parameter for the fit. (This is the same as the "Regularization Parameter"
    setting in the GUI).


--shape_control
    Shape control for the radial basis function. Options are "fwhm" or "shape"
    to use the FWHM coefficient or the shape factor to control the radial basis functions.
    (This is the same as the "RBF Shape Control" setting in the GUI).

--coefficient
    Coefficient for the FWHM control of the radial basis functions.
    (This is the same as the "FWHM Control" setting in the GUI).

--sample_number
    Number of samples to use in the analysis. (This is the same as the
    "Number of Sammples" setting in the GUI).

You can get more information about the required and optional arguments for the script
by calling the script with the "--help" option:

    python headless_pyDRTtools.py --help

Outputs
-------
The script outputs the specified plots (and their corresponding data)
within a folder named "DRT" within the "input_dir" as passed to the script.
Each of the plots will be named identically to the data file from which it is derived,
but has a suffix naming the type of plot and the method used to run the DRT.
